WEBVTT

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.820
The news has gotten kind of confusing.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.680
I don't know if I'm allowed to admit that as a person who talks here on the public radio.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.440
It's confusing to me, especially all this stuff about the trouble the banks are in.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.640
You know, you turn on the Today Show at random and you can find yourself watching something like this.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.860
As of this date, this is a static date, all these large banks exceed regulatory standards for being well capitalized.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.760
So for right now, they're fine.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.540
That's the chairman of the Federal Deposit and Insurance Corporation, Sheila Baer.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.620
and this is mainstream network television in the morning.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.760
God help you if you accidentally flip over to CNBC.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.500
So many of these banks, the stocks are telling you the underlying equity value is zero, or maybe a little bit above zero.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.860
Wouldn't mark-to-market accounting, a hiatus from mark-to-market, do that exact same thing?

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.320
Okay, I'm just going to stop the tape right there.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.440
I mean, come on.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.860
That's not even trying to help us understand.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:01:04.860
Even the Obama administration, whose very jobs depend on us understanding what they're doing to try to fix the economy, who are actually trying to explain this stuff to us, even they have these moments.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:13.300
Those institutions that need additional capital will be able to access a new funding mechanism that uses capital from the Treasury as a bridge to private capital.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.420
This is Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner on Capitol Hill explaining his plan to fix our banking system.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:30.220
The capital will come with conditions to help ensure that every dollar of taxpayer assistance is being used to generate a level of lending greater than what would have been possible in the absence of government support.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.000
And this assistance will come with terms...

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:33.940
Forget it.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.300
Here's what I understand, what I think most of us understand.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:40.960
Stock market's way down.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:42.430
It seems to be dropping.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:49.460
Banks aren't lending, even though the government has given them hundreds of billions of dollars of our money to help them start lending again.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:59.460
And my life, your life, the entire economic fate of our country and the world for the next decade depends on whether or not the United States can fix its banking system.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:07.440
And maybe you're on the verge of just giving up, of figuring that this is just going to be one of those news stories that you're just going to kind of sit out, you know?

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:09.980
I sat out Kosovo.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.220
I'm not proud about that fact, but I did.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:13.340
did.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.500
Well, if that's your situation, I have good news for you.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:24.560
The team that put together our show explaining the mortgage crisis last year that so many of you wrote to us to let us know that you found so helpful.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.460
This is the team, Adam Davidson from NPR News, NPR economics correspondent, and This American Life producer, Alex Bloomberg.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:32.620
They are back.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:33.360
They are back today.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.320
And in the next hour, they are going to explain everything you need to know to understand the U.S.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.800
banking And how it might or might not be fixed.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.020
And I got to say, they've been working on this all week.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:45.980
I've heard the thing.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:46.790
It is awesome.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:47.670
It is awesome.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.600
And at the end of it, you will actually be able to have an opinion.

