From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.760
Today's show is another collaboration between us and NPR News.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.580
The collapse of the U.S.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.420
banking system explained in just 59 minutes.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.020
Stay with us.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.960
And with that, I turn things over into the capable hands of Adam and Alex.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.260
If you want to understand this crisis right now, this banking crisis, you need to understand this one thing.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.020
And it's one thing, Adam, that the mainstream media is afraid to touch.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.500
They're afraid because they think it's really boring.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:30.980
Right.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:31.980
Right.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.500
Because what this central thing is, this thing that we need to discuss right now, is a bank balance sheet.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.980
But please do not despair, because we think we've come up with a way to explain this to you, and we actually think it'll be pretty enjoyable.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.800
So, to begin, let's imagine the simplest bank in the world.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.940
I would like to call it Adam's Bank.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.400
How come you're always the bank owner in these imaginary scenarios?

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.800
I mean, Alex, I just think of myself as more like a bank owner, and you more like a bank customer.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:01.760
I see.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.260
Okay, Mr. Fancy Pants, go ahead.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:04.620
Okay.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.580
So I have $10 of my own money that I'm going to start my bank with.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.240
So I take these $10 and I open for business.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:21.600
I start accepting deposits and I go to my good friend and colleague, Alex Bloomberg, and I say, hey, Alex, you want to open a savings deposit with my bank?

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.200
I'll give you 3% interest a year.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.900
I say, that sounds great.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.240
I actually have $90 right here that I'm saving for a rainy day.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.640
And so I will hand it over to you.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:34.440
Here you go.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:37.920
$80, $90.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:38.560
There you go.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:38.920
All right.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:39.220
Okay.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.680
So now I have $100 in my bag, $10 of which is mine, $90 I got from Alex.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:46.020
All right.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.020
And so right now this is a lousy business.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.860
I'm losing money because I'm paying Alex 3% interest.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.460
I'm not getting anything coming in.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.440
I need to make some profit, and that requires one other person.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:58.300
Hi, guys.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.240
Oh, hey, Caitlin.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:04:59.740
Hey, Caitlin.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.240
This is Caitlin Kenney.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.580
She's a Planet Money producer.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:03.960
Hey, what's up?

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:04.360
What's going on?

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.810
I need to borrow $100.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:07.560
For what?

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:08.560
To buy a house.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:10.800
You can get a house for $100?

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:11.940
It's a dollhouse.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.120
Oh, actually, this is perfect.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.660
So I can give you, Caitlin, the $100 I have in my bank, but on one condition.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:49.860
you have to pay me back at a higher interest rate than what i'm paying alex so uh that way i can make some money so let's say six percent sounds good okay here you go all right that's a hundred dollars awesome later wait remember you gotta pay me six percent a year huh the mortgage you gotta go yeah sure got it no problem all right yeah i'm in business this is great i'm getting Getting 6% from Caitlin, I pay 3% to Alex.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.360
I get to keep the difference.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:51.900
Okay.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.260
And at a fundamental level, banking is really this simple.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.700
You pay your depositors, in this case me, a low rate, and then you lend out money at a higher rate.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:03.080
You keep the difference.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.760
In fact, there's even an old banking joke.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.220
Banking is a 3-6-3 business.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:14.020
You take in money at 3%, you lend it out at 6%, you're on the golf course by 3 in the afternoon.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:19.820
Oh, bankers.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.360
Oh, bankers.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.020
And this brings us to the subject of today's radio drama.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:29.920
Correct.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.660
So what we have here right now is the world's simplest bank balance sheet.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.500
Picture a piece of paper with a line drawn down the middle.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:45.800
On the right side is my $10, the $10 I started the bank with, and the $90 I got from Alex.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.060
On the left side is the $100 I gave to Caitlin.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.000
If you notice, both sides are equal.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:57.380
90 plus 10 on the right side equals 100 on the left side.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.880
And that's why they call it a balance sheet.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.260
Both sides have to balance.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.520
Okay, now some jargon.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.080
Adam's $10, that's called the bank's capital.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.100
The people who own the bank, that is what they own.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:19.160
And when Adam makes his profit every year, you know, the difference between what he's getting from Caitlin and he's giving to me, that profit is added to the capital.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.720
So year over year, if the bank is well run, the capital gets bigger, Adam gets richer.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.160
But now the jargon gets completely confusing and backwards because most of us aren't used to thinking like a bank.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:37.340
The 90 bucks that I deposited in Adam's bank, that also has a name and it's called Adam's liabilities.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.080
Now to me, of course, it's not a liability.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:40.730
It's a deposit.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:41.840
It's my savings.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.660
But to Adam, it's a liability because if you think about it, he owes me that 90 bucks.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.300
He's liable to me for that money.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:53.800
I can withdraw it at any time and he needs to pay me interest on it.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.900
To him, it's like a loan I gave him.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.020
And I think we should introduce another piece of jargon.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:07:59.240
Sure.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:04.280
That hundred bucks that I gave to Caitlin, that's called my asset.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:04.760
Huh?

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.320
Well, you have to pay me some money every month.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:35.260
So I think of caitlin's dollhouse mortgage i think of it like an investment i gave you 100 bucks up front you pay me back six percent a year for 30 years or however long your dollhouse mortgages that is my asset because you pay me that nice return every year right huh you're gonna pay me six percent a year right oh i said that yes you you have a mortgage okay but excellent work guys that was very nicely illustrated.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:36.060
Okay.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:40.420
So the point is every single balance sheet looks this way.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.630
You got on the one side, on the left side, you have assets.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.640
And on the right side, you have liabilities plus capital.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.700
And they always balance out.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:59.860
And this is true from this very simple hypothetical bank in a world with one depositor and one dollhouse to the largest and most complicated bank that has ever existed, Citibank, whose balance sheet I have right here.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.260
And Alex, you did not like secretly break into the corporate offices of Citibank, right?

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.800
That's something you can just print up off the internet.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:08.020
Right.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:09.340
And it says here what they have.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:16.360
Instead of $100 in assets, of course, they have a little bit more, specifically $1.95 trillion in assets.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:17.580
That's a lot of dollhouses.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:18.120
Yeah, it is.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:19.360
And I'm reading it right here.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:21.980
It says assets, total assets, $1.95 trillion.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.240
And then on the other side, liabilities, $1.8 trillion.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:28.680
Capital, $150 billion.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.540
and that also equals $1.95 trillion.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.020
It all adds up.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.400
Assets equal liabilities plus capital, both sides of the balance sheet balance.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.220
So here's where things get interesting.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:45.560
And here's why we're explaining bank balance sheets on the radio and why you are, we hope, listening with rapt attention.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:48.720
Alex, I really hope they're still with us.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:49.000
Me too.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:49.640
I hope you're out there.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:50.740
Okay, it's getting interesting.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.800
It all has to do with Caitlin.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:54.820
Hey, Adam.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:55.400
Hey, Caitlin.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.320
I can't pay my mortgage.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:58.300
What the?

00:09:58.900 --> 00:09:59.920
I lost my job.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:04.580
I mean, you didn't ask me about my job in the first place, but now I don't have one.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.020
So that money, it's not coming.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:08.860
Oh, my God.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:14.140
Well, I mean, you know, for a few years there, you bankers were giving out loans to all sorts of deadbeats like Caitlin.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:14.420
Hey!

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:15.820
Sorry, Caitlin.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:17.620
Without checking to see if they had jobs first.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:20.500
All right, I'm going to kick you out and take the dollhouse.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.430
But my baby, my baby!

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.000
Where will my baby sleep?

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:25.660
Caitlin, it's a doll baby.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:27.140
Get out.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:27.960
Fine.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:48.740
Okay, so now Adam's bank owns Caitlin's dollhouse and he wants to sell it, but it's a down market in dollhouses and all the dollhouses out there, just like Caitlin's, are only selling for $95, which is a problem for your balance sheet, Adam, because now it looks like this.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:59.480
Liabilities, 90, that you owe me, 10 of your own dollars equals 100, but on the other side, assets, one dollhouse worth $95.

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:01.320
Your balance sheet is out of balance.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:02.640
So I'm a good banker.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.720
I know obviously the balance sheet has to balance.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.780
So that $90, that's Alex's money, Alex's deposit.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:15.500
That can't change because if he wants to withdraw it at a moment's notice, I have to be ready to give him 90 bucks at any time.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:23.180
The only thing that can change on that side of the balance sheet is my capital, the $10 of my own money I started out with.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:26.240
So now my balance sheet looks like this.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:29.580
On the asset side, one dollhouse worth $95.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:37.560
On the liability side, $90 deposit from Alex, my liability, $5 in capital, my money.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.600
So both sides equal 95.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:40.820
They're back in balance.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.840
But with the stroke of an accountant's pen, I've lost five of my own dollars.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.800
I've lost half my money.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:47.780
This really sucks.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:54.900
In this situation I'm in with Caitlin, this is the situation a lot of our biggest banks are in right now, but much worse.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:56.120
Right.

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.240
Because instead of one Caitlyn, there are millions of them.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:03.780
And they all bought dollhouses at the height of a dollhouse mania.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:11.280
And dollhouses have lost not 5% of their value, but 10%, 20%, sometimes 50%, depending on where they live.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:15.380
They've gone from being assets to toxic assets.

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:17.220
That's probably another term you've heard.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:25.440
So if Caitlyn's dollhouse is in fact a toxic asset, if her dollhouse has dropped in price, let's say by 50%. Let's go back to our balance sheet.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:35.020
If the bank, you Adam, takes over Caitlin's dollhouse in this situation and can sell it for only 50% of its value, then your balance sheet looks very bad.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:39.860
A $50 loss on the left side of the balance sheet, which has to be matched on the right side.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.500
So my $10, my capital, that's totally gone.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:46.360
And 40 of my $90 is now gone as well.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:51.010
So not Not only is my bank wiped out, but I can't even pay back my creditors.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.880
In this case, my good friend and colleague, Mr. Alex Bloomberg.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:55.740
And this is, of course, a bad thing.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:56.870
I lose half my savings.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.280
Adam loses his bank.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.760
It's all because of Caitlin and her stupid dollhouse.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:01.480
Oops.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:04.500
Is that all you have to say?

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:05.900
Sorry.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:06.880
All right.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:07.960
There might be a way out.

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:08.540
Yeah.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.180
I don't want this whole scenario to happen.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:11.760
And so I come up with a plan.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:13.900
I'm not going to sell Caitlin's dollhouse.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.060
But what about my money?

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:17.820
Isn't it only worth half of what she paid for it?

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:18.540
You have to sell it.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.040
I need to get something back, right?

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:21.860
Hey, Alex, don't worry about that.

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:26.360
Your money is totally safe in my bank because let me tell you something.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:28.760
The dollhouse market is coming back.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:35.660
And all I have to do is just keep Caitlin's dollhouse on my balance sheet for more or less its original value, somewhere around $100.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.180
And I have a simple claim.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.820
The market for dollhouses is illiquid right now.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:45.460
In other words, I am not able to sell the dollhouse into this market.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.720
so there's no way to determine a market price.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:49.040
We're fine.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:54.680
We just have to assume the market is going to come back and I can sell it for $100 down the road.

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:56.400
But that's a crazy assumption.

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:58.620
We're entering maybe the worst recession in decades.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:00.500
People are losing their jobs all over the place.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.330
Everyone says things are probably going to get worse in the short term, not better.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:03.640
Shh!

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:11.140
Alex, I've chosen to believe that this house will be worth more one day.

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:12.720
Can you let me have my dream?

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:17.480
If we just assume the dollhouse hasn't gone down in value, everything is okay.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.880
It's funny, I talked to a professor at Columbia Business School.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:25.260
He's an expert in bank crises and a former banker himself, David Bimes is his name.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:26.820
He told me he'd say that.

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:31.340
Most often, the bankers say, well, it's not as bad as that.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:41.940
For example, in the current crisis, many banks hold these so-called toxic assets whose quoted prices are down around 20 or 30 percent of their face value.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:42.060
you.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.140
And the bankers say, look, I'm just sure it's worth more than that.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.720
I don't want to market to market.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:47.380
Okay.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.440
That last phrase that he just said, let's listen again.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:51.560
I don't want to market to market.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:56.800
David Byman is saying, you don't want to mark it to market.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:14:58.740
Mark to market.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:00.380
That's another phrase you might've heard.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:03.460
And it applies to exactly the situation Adam is in right now.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:10.140
He's got a dollhouse on his books for a hundred, but if he had to sell it now, he could only get 50. That's the market price, what he could get right now.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:19.160
Marking it to market means Adam would have to enter the market price, $50 or $20 or whatever it really is, into his books.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:24.440
And the bankers have all been saying, please don't make me do that, because if you do, I'll be declaring bankruptcy.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:31.560
If I show all those as a reduction from 100 all the way down to 20, you've just wiped out my entire capital and more.

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.180
I'm going to have to go to the government and say, close me down, I'm broke.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:36.760
And bankers find that very hard to do.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.580
And furthermore, regulators don't really want that to happen to all the banks at once.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:42.080
Certainly not all the big ones.

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:46.960
Now, obviously, in the real world, the assets that banks have on their books are more complicated than dollhouses.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:56.620
But if the banks had to sell them now in today's market, they'd almost certainly take a huge loss, a loss big enough to wipe out their capital and shut them down.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:58.380
And it's not just a few banks.

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:05.040
Banking experts estimate that more than a thousand banks banks are either facing this situation right now or they will soon.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:10.040
And that includes, and this is the crucial point, many of America's largest banks.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.160
They owe more money than they have.

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:15.550
And there's a word for this, insolvent.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:18.540
You don't have to take our word for it.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:25.560
One of our colleagues on the Planet Money team, David Kestenbaum, talked to Jeremy Siegel, who is, it's true, called the Wizard of Wharton.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.660
Wharton, of course, being that famous business school.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:34.400
How many of the banks right now do you think would be insolvent if someone, on, as you said, hard-headed went in and valued what they actually have?

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:38.960
Well, I don't know how many, but I think there might be on a current market value.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:55.500
And again, the market may be over-discounting some of these, but probably wouldn't be surprised if Citi and Bank America really at current value of their assets don't cover the depositors and the bond holders and would wipe out shareholders' equity.

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:56.700
And there could be others too.

00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:58.480
All right, let's just pause there for one moment.

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:02.340
When they talk about the banking crisis, This is what they mean.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:03.000
This is it.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:08.940
Siegel is saying two of the biggest banks in the United States, their assets are too small to cover their liabilities.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:16.140
The losses wipe out the capital, and there's still not enough left to pay for all the depositors and other people who lent the banks money.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:17.440
That is just huge.

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:19.740
Yeah, this quote really makes me nervous.

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:21.880
Though I do want to be clear about something.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:38.060
Normal people, depositors like Alex with a regular savings account, they would not lose any money under any scenario because the government guarantees their deposits up up to $250,000, but big banks get a lot of their money from big investors who lend them millions or billions of dollars.

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:39.740
And that money is not protected.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:43.660
If the banks go down, these investors could lose their money.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:46.680
And the consequences of that will ripple around the world.

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:51.040
Of course, Citibank and Bank of America dispute the claim that they're insolvent.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.580
They both say they have more than enough capital and there's no need to worry.

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:59.720
And we should say the Treasury Department and federal regulators say that's true, that they have more than enough capital.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.240
But it seems that the market does not believe this story.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:04.620
Consider this fact.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:10.530
Citibank claims on its balance sheet that its worth, its own capital, is more than $150 billion.

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:23.520
But if you add up all the stock out there, which is how you find out what the market thinks Citibank is worth, here, I'm just going to type C into Google, that's the code for Citibank stock, and wow.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:24.380
Wow. Okay.

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:34.400
As of Friday morning, when we're recording it, Citibank's total value, according to the stock market, the world's investors, is less than $10 billion.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:37.960
That's actually about the size of the Heineken beer company.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:40.760
Less than the size of the Heineken beer company.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:43.940
And Citibank historically has been one of the largest companies in the world.

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:51.300
So what you see here is two stories, what the banks are saying about their balance sheets and what people outside the banks are saying.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.220
The government, by the way, is siding with the banks for now.

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:56.440
They believe that the dollhouse value will come back.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:59.480
They believe that if we just wait a little while, the banks will be fine.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:00.060
Right.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:06.200
Which is important to know because what you do about the banking crisis depends on which version of the truth you believe.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:14.200
The Secretary of the Treasury and the federal government say now anyway, that Citibank and all the other major banks are well capitalized and they just need a little cushion for insurance.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:15.240
All right.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:17.040
So this is the situation our country is in.

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:25.800
If the Wizard of Wharton and the stock market are right, two of our largest banks are either insolvent or really, really close, and probably lots of others as well.

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:33.160
Those two banks, Citibank and Bank of America alone, have over a quarter of all the money in the U.S.

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:33.460
banking system.

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:38.540
90% of all the money anybody has on deposit in the U.S.

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:40.250
is just in the 20 largest banks.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:46.900
So if they start falling, the US economy, the world economy, are basically done for a while.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.260
So what do we do?

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:52.080
There are two options that have been discussed a lot.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:20:02.780
One option was the one that former Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson originally proposed, the first TARP, which you'll remember stood for Troubled Asset Relief Program.

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:04.520
And now that word might make a little bit more sense.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:06.380
The idea there is pretty simple.

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:10.220
You take Caitlin's dollhouse as a typical troubled asset.

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:12.580
It's selling right now for only $50.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:18.900
Under this plan, the government agrees with Adam that someday the market will pay a lot more for that dollhouse.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:24.360
And so it buys it from Adam for a higher price than he could get right now.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:25.670
Let's say $92.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:26.880
Okay.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:29.260
I'd still take a hit to my capital.

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:30.940
I'd lose a lot of my money.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:37.480
I'd lose eight of my 10 bucks, but I'd get to keep my bank and I'd keep my depositors like you, Alex, looks whole.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:43.500
But we, the taxpayers, get stuck with a $50 dollhouse that we paid almost $100 for.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.880
And if dollhouse prices don't go up, the government turns out to be a pretty big sucker.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:56.020
And this plan bails out people like Adam, even though he's the guy who got us into this crisis, by making unbelievably stupid loans to deadbeats like Caitlyn.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:56.800
Easy, pal.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:57.540
Easy, easy.

00:20:58.040 --> 00:20:59.020
Sorry, guys, I got carried away.

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:09.000
Anyway, the government can't pay less for the dollhouse, can't get a a better deal because if it gets a good deal, if it only pays what the market would pay, that wouldn't save the banks.

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:10.080
That would defeat the whole purpose.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:15.060
Now, I just want to say that I, as a banker, I love this plan.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:18.960
I think it's brilliant because sure, I'll lose some money, but I'm still in business.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:19.860
I'm solvent.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:21.140
And you know what?

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:21.740
It's not just me.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.900
All my banker friends think this plan is brilliant.

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:26.640
I talked to Simon Johnson.

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:27.460
He's an economist.

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:29.820
He used to be with the International Monetary Fund.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:35.660
Now he's with at the Peterson Institute, and he found a good example of another banker giving the hard sell for this plan.

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:39.460
So you brought us this piece of paper today.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.000
This is having you laugh.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:42.040
Can you tell me what this is?

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:43.920
Well, it's a note from Deutsche Bank.

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:45.540
It's part of their U.S.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:46.560
Daily Economic Notes.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:49.500
This is some staff economist there that sends out every day.

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:49.700
That's right.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:53.220
And they send these around to clients and to people that they want to have conversations with.

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:54.020
Of course, it goes to government.

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:55.920
These are very influential, these kinds of things.

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:56.020
things.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:00.720
So this research note, can I just plunge in to say what's in it?

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:00.840
Yeah.

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:03.300
The title is eye-catching.

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:08.200
It's called Falling Short, colon, The Government Needs to Buy Toxic Assets.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:09.600
So it's a very straight statement.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:10.980
It obviously speaks to the issues of the week.

00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:13.400
And the paragraph, it's a one-pager.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:34.100
And the paragraph, or the bolded text, they bolded it, that really struck me and I think summarizes the tone of it is, it says, ultimately, the taxpayer will pay one way or another, either through greatly diminished job prospects and or significantly higher taxes down the line, we think the government should do the following.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:43.120
Estimate the highest price it can pay for the various toxic assets residing on financial institution balance sheets, which would still return the principal to the taxpayers.

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.680
Now, if I can cut through the, if I can translate that.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:47.460
You have a phrase for this note.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:49.820
This is a robbery note.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:23:03.120
It's saying, saying, guys, either you'll have 20% unemployment or national debt will go up to these dangerous levels unless you buy toxic assets, not for what they're worth, not for what the market price is, as much as you can pay.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:03.720
Wow.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:08.260
So it is saying, I mean, the key line here is the taxpayer will pay one way or another.

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:18.180
I had a landlord, I don't think he was in the mafia, but he tried to cultivate the image and he would, you know, he'd say things like, there's an easy way and there's a hard way.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:21.920
And I can just hear hear him saying that you're going to pay one way or another.

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:28.900
And so basically what this note is saying is, look guys, you're going to hurt either way.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:30.200
Give us the money now.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:31.640
We'll try and make it easy for you.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:34.180
My first reaction, my first reaction was, it's a spoof.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:44.280
My second reaction was, oh my God. We figured Simon should argue it out with the Deutsche Bank guy, Joe LaVornia.

00:23:47.060 --> 00:23:47.820
Joe LaVornia.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:48.460
Hey, Joe.

00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:49.860
It's Adam Davidson from NPR.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:50.300
Hi.

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:50.520
Hi.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:52.250
So I'm on the line with Simon Johnson.

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:56.800
Your note today, did you write it or you wrote it with your staff?

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:57.740
Me.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:23:58.860
You wrote it.

00:23:58.900 --> 00:23:59.100
Right.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:23:59.900
Yep, I wrote it.

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:02.500
Simon was actually really nervous about calling Joe.

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:05.700
He thought he'd get all mad at us for saying this was a robbery note.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:06.640
But Joe was cool.

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:12.640
I think the bottom line is simply someone has to pay for the mess that's been created.

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:17.460
and there's no escaping the taxpayers on the hawks.

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:24.640
And let me just say, Joe Livornia is finally coming out and saying something that every other bank and lots of government people have avoided saying.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:33.000
They've been playing this game, saying there's some magical recipe where the government bails out the banks, the banks do better, the taxpayers end up making money, everyone wins.

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:36.760
And this note is saying what we keep hearing from economists.

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:38.640
That probably can't happen.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:40.240
Someone is going to lose.

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:42.860
And Joe is saying he knows exactly who that's going to be.

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:46.900
You and me and everybody who pays taxes.

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:52.800
I think, Joe, I found it refreshingly honest, but it also kind of took my breath away.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:57.130
And the reaction, so I put it out there and I asked people what they thought on my blog report.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:24:58.300
I didn't use names.

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:04.960
I just put out this key paragraph that you were just discussing and the key issue that taxpayers will pay one way or another.

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:05.060
another.

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:10.940
So one guy said, quote, he's sort of paraphrasing how he read the note.

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:14.560
He said, that sure is a nice global economy you've got there.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:18.080
Be a shame if anything happened to it.

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:20.680
And Joe, I got to say what I love about your note.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:22.540
Do you mind if we call it a ransom note?

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:23.860
Oh, I wouldn't prefer it.

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:29.900
If I was on my own, I would say fine, but I wouldn't say a ransom note.

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:31.300
I would say a reality check.

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:34.080
I mean, the thing is, I think Simon's exactly right.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:34.960
And this is the issue.

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:39.500
We're delaying the pain and you got to just, you got to deal with the problem.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:41.560
And I guess the issue is just dealing with the problem.

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.760
What we've done to this point has just simply not been aggressive enough.

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:49.180
So whatever, whatever the, whatever the approach is, let's just, let's just get there.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:01.860
So let's talk about a second way the government could address this this whole mess.

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:05.940
The government could say to bankers like me, okay, dude, get real.

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:09.480
I would have to recognize my loss on Caitlyn's dollhouse.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:13.200
So I just have to say, yes, I am $50 in the hole.

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:19.580
So my capital is wiped out and I owe my depositor, you know, Alex, 40 bucks.

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:26.300
But then under this second plan, the government comes in on the other side of my balance sheet and covers my losses.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:33.560
It gives $40 for Alex and it replaces the $10 in capital that got wiped out that I lost.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:43.340
Now, the problem here is that if the government puts in that much capital, then the government now owns the bank, my bank.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:48.780
And I want to say as a bank owner, I do not like this plan because I would lose my money.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:49.960
I'd lose my job.

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:52.900
And, you know, I'm a banker.

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:55.760
I don't like this kind of government takeover of private industry.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:56.820
It's nationalization.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:57.860
I hate nationalization.

00:26:58.220 --> 00:26:59.760
This seems like socialism to me.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:07.660
Yeah, but at least this way, I, as a taxpayer, have an ownership stake in a bank, not some crappy, overvalued dollhouse.

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:09.560
And the government won't just own it forever.

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:13.940
It'll clean the bank up, sell it to someone else down the road, and maybe we'll get some of our money back.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:17.240
And frankly, I like the fact that you lose your job.

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:18.420
You got us into this mess.

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:19.040
All right.

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:21.180
Oh, no, I hurt your feelings.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:21.520
I'm sorry.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.760
OK, I wish you could keep your job.

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:25.240
But don't you understand what I'm saying?

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:26.180
You know, I'm mad.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:26.640
We're all mad.

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:31.040
And this is the traditional way governments usually fix banks.

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:36.000
For the last 20 years, Simon Johnson has worked on banking crises all over the world.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:44.480
He used to be the chief economist for the International Monetary Fund, an organization that stepped in over and over around the globe to fix just the kind of crisis we're in right now.

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:50.220
And the IMF, with the United States, pushed countries in similar situations to do what?

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:52.120
To nationalize their banks temporarily.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:52.740
temporarily.

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:58.600
Indonesia, 97. Korea, 97, 98. Russia, every couple of years.

00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:00.240
Argentina, 2002.

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:04.460
What would the US tell the IMF to do if this were any country other than the US?

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:15.160
If you covered up the name of the country and just show me the numbers, just show me the problems, just talk to me a little bit about the politics in a generic way, I know what we would do.

00:28:15.220 --> 00:28:16.300
I know what the advice would be.

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:22.400
And that would be take over the banking system, clean it up, Reprivatize it as soon as you can.

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:26.480
So then the logical follow-up question is, why?

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:30.280
Why is the United States not taking its own advice?

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:33.760
That is a great question, a huge question.

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:37.280
My take is that it's too political.

00:28:37.400 --> 00:28:38.160
The politics are awkward.

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:42.760
Remember, cleaning up banking systems, in my view, technically, is not that difficult.

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:47.060
But when you clean up a banking system, you do it properly, some powerful people lose.

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:48.400
They lose their bonuses.

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:49.360
They lose their banks.

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:50.440
They lose their access.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:51.940
So who's going to lose?

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:54.080
Who's going to decide who's in and who's out?

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:57.520
I don't think the people at the top are yet ready to have that conversation.

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:03.580
One of the reasons they might not be ready to have that conversation has to do with the practical challenges of taking over the banks.

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:07.120
Columbia Business School professor David Byam knows about this.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.540
He has experience himself taking over American banks, assisting the U.S.

00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:10.740
government.

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:16.020
This was back during our last banking crisis, the savings and loan crisis in the late 1980s.

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:20.260
When that crisis hit, he started advising the FDIC on the problem.

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:21.780
which was big.

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:25.960
Every large bank in Texas and Oklahoma failed in that era, every one of them.

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:31.780
It was the biggest banking crisis America had faced since the Great Depression, and compared to now, it was nothing.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:47.140
What I faced in the 80s was a regional crisis, so that the FDIC was willing to shut every major bank in Texas and Oklahoma, cleanse them of their bad loans, relaunch them with new management and new shareholders.

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:53.780
That was the best practice of of the day, and they did it very well, and they got through the mess and got through it elegantly.

00:29:54.980 --> 00:29:57.420
It's harder to do that for the nation.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:01.660
It's harder to say all the big banks in the country should now be closed.

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:04.360
That is kind of awesome.

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:07.060
I mean, you would not want to do that all at once.

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:11.120
This crisis has advanced so fast and so globally.

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:13.220
It's not just the United States, of course.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:15.120
Banks are crashing all over Europe.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:20.300
The banks of Europe are actually in worse shape than those of the the United States, even those that never touched a mortgage-backed security.

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:22.600
This is a global crisis.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:24.340
It's absolutely everywhere.

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:30.840
And under those circumstances, it's harder to advocate closing down all the banks.

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:33.900
But I'm trying to understand, what is the difficult?

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:36.940
Is it simply you need somebody to sell the assets to?

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:41.060
And if you're taking over the largest banks in the country, there's nobody to sell assets to?

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:41.860
Is that what it is?

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.590
Yeah, you can sell one bank.

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:49.660
You know, I'm sure you could put some buyers together, there's private equity funds and others who could, I'm sure, be found to buy one bank.

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:54.700
But to buy the banking system of the globe is sort of a tall order.

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:06.320
There's another practical challenge to taking over all of our insolvent banks.

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:09.560
The government might just not have enough people to do it.

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:14.100
The one time the FDIC actually fully nationalized a U.S.

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:19.720
bank, which was Continental Illinois in 1984, it took more than 100 government regulators.

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:22.860
Citibank alone is 20 times bigger.

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:26.120
And the banking system is far more complicated now than it was then.

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:31.840
One expert told me we might be talking about thousands of people needed for each bank we take over.

00:31:32.860 --> 00:31:33.500
A second problem?

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:36.360
Nationalizations are kind of like potato chips.

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:37.900
It's hard to have just one.

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:46.200
you'd have to come out with a plan for all of them or all the big banks, and you'd probably have to do the whole thing in one day at one time.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:58.100
Because if you just take over one big bank, people with money at the other banks will start worrying that their bank will be nationalized next, and that'll cause investors to panic and they'll just pull all their money out of the bank.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:00.920
Now, to be fair, there's debate about these logistical arguments.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:08.940
Simon Johnson, the economist who worked at the IMF, says he heard very practical arguments against nationalization in every country he went into.

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:12.260
And there are ways to deal with all of it, the timing, the staffing.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:18.960
He said it might be possible, for example, for the government to hold onto the banks for just a few hours before turning it back over to private investors.

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:25.620
And so when bankers like me tell Simon that there's all these practical reasons we can't do this in America, he has an answer.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:33.540
I'm sure they're all sensible, Adam, but let me speak as if I were the IMF acting on the behalf and with the support of the U.S.

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:34.840
government back in the 1990s.

00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:37.140
Adam, stop with the whining.

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:38.940
You know it's got to be done.

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:40.320
Just do it.

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:45.460
The longer you wait, the more you prevaricate, the more it's going to cost you.

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:46.760
This is the U.S.

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:47.520
government we're talking about.

00:32:47.940 --> 00:32:51.320
These people, when they get organized, when they get focused, they get things done.

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:54.540
This is the greatest country ever in the face of the earth.

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:55.940
We have plenty of talent.

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:57.420
There's no shortage of brainpower.

00:32:58.000 --> 00:32:58.440
Just do it.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:09.280
Now, you might have noticed that the government is not doing either of these options.

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:12.060
Instead, they're doing sort of halfway versions of both.

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:18.700
They have not committed to buying up all the dollhouses and other toxic assets at something closer to their full price.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:27.460
But they have created a new trial program that allows the government to help subsidize private investors who they hope will buy a lot of these assets.

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:45.020
And when it comes to option two, the government is not forcing the banks to sell their assets or value them at what they could get on the market right now.

00:33:45.560 --> 00:33:47.980
The government is not taking over the banks.

00:33:48.100 --> 00:33:49.280
They're not nationalizing them.

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:55.600
In fact, the government has gone out of its way to give banks money without taking control.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:55.900
control.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:34:02.020
They've given banks over $240 billion, including $45 billion to Citibank alone.

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:08.880
But the government structured the deal in a special way, specifically designed so it was not a nationalization.

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:17.660
And this week, when Citibank's troubles got worse, the government had to go through these amazing contortions to help the bank without becoming its owner.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:26.640
Because the government, and all of them from President Bush and Henry Paulson to President Obama and Tim Tim Geithner and Ben Bernanke, they all say the same thing.

00:34:27.160 --> 00:34:29.460
They don't want the government owning banks.

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:33.980
Now, Alex, I always think that there is another option.

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:37.220
The government could just let the banking system sort things out on its own.

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:39.720
If banks made bad bets, then they go out of business.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:40.700
It's not our problem.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:50.920
Well, the Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke dismissed this option when he told Congress in a private briefing that it actually is our problem.

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:51.020
him.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:59.020
He was quoted as saying, if we let the banking system fail, no one will talk about the Great Depression anymore because this will be so much worse.

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:06.800
So we need banks, which Columbia Business School professor David Byrne says is why governments always protect them.

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:10.460
The year 37 in ancient Rome, there was a bank run.

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:22.680
They had a very sophisticated banking system and there was a run on those banks in AD 37. Some number of ships didn't come in with their cargo or something something happened and the emperor was out of Rome.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:24.100
And how did he deal with it?

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:26.600
He did just what Ben Bernanke would do.

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:30.960
He came galloping back into Rome with bags full of coin and distributed them to the banks.

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:32.880
It's been happening as long as there have been banks.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:34.040
Governments must protect banks.

00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:46.000
So if the government won't let these banks fail and also won't take them over, it means that Citibank, Bank of America, throw in JPMorgan Chase and three or four others, they have us over a barrel.

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:47.920
We can't let them just fail.

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:50.740
And it's going to cost us one way or another to keep them alive.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:52.720
And this sucks.

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:56.600
I feel that, you feel that, Congress definitely feels that.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:59.360
Or at least constituents are communicating those feelings to Congress.

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:05.220
And this frustration leads to a lot of lashing out about almost exactly the wrong thing.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:10.100
Here's Massachusetts Congressman Mike Capuano yelling at the banks.

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:13.040
Start loaning the money that we gave you.

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:15.920
Get it on the street.

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:22.760
This might be a reasonable expectation for healthy banks, but for insolvent banks, it can be a disaster.

00:36:23.540 --> 00:36:25.240
And the reason all goes back to our balance sheet.

00:36:25.940 --> 00:36:29.840
When a bank is insolvent, it doesn't have the capital to cover its losses.

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:34.880
In that situation, banks would actually be doing the right thing by keeping the bailout money that we're giving them.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:39.580
They need to hold on to their capital, and that's how they fix their balance sheets.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:44.880
If they loaned the money away, they'd be returning to the situation we are trying to rescue them from.

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:50.660
In other words, saving the banking system means that the banks that are worse off should loan less, not more.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:57.300
But beyond the balance sheet, David Byam has a much more profound reason why banks shouldn't lend.

00:36:58.120 --> 00:36:59.620
He shows me something on his computer.

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:02.100
Okay, so here is a picture.

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:03.620
Okay, this is a graphic.

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:11.600
We're in his office and we're looking at a graph, which is basically a measure of how much debt we, the citizens of America, are in.

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:18.200
how much we all owe on our mortgages and credit cards and auto loans compared to the economy as a whole, the GDP.

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:24.580
And for most of history, the amount we as Americans owed was a lot smaller than the economy as a whole.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:33.460
This ratio, household debt to GDP, bounces along between 30% and 50% for most of the 30s, 40s, 50s, 60s, 70s.

00:37:34.140 --> 00:37:36.840
It breaks through 50% in the 80s, and then...

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:40.660
From 2000 to 2008, it just goes...

00:37:40.660 --> 00:37:42.180
almost a hockey stick.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:43.620
It goes dramatically upward.

00:37:43.820 --> 00:37:56.540
It hits 100% of GDP. That is to say, currently, consumers owe $13 trillion when the GDP is $13 trillion.

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:01.840
That's a 100% of GDP owed by individuals.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:02.740
That is a ton.

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:07.940
I'm going to ask you a leading question because I'm looking at the graph right now.

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:11.940
Tell me, Professor, has there ever been a time in history when we've owned that much before?

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:13.320
Well, I'm glad you asked me that.

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:14.360
And guess what?

00:38:14.540 --> 00:38:29.700
The earlier peak, way off on the left part of the chart, is 100% of GDP in 1929. This is a map of twin peaks, one in 1929, one in 2007. Does that chart scare you?

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:40.580
Yes. That chart is the most striking piece piece of evidence that I have that what is happening to us is something that goes way beyond toxic assets in banks.

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:49.040
It's something that has little to do with the mechanics of mortgage securitization or ethics on Wall Street or anything else.

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:51.380
It says the problem is us.

00:38:52.640 --> 00:38:58.060
The problem is not the banks, greedy though they may be, overpaid though they may be.

00:38:58.160 --> 00:38:59.720
The problem is us.

00:39:00.500 --> 00:39:01.880
We have over-borrowed.

00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:04.440
We've been living very high on the hog.

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:14.480
Our standard of living has been rising dramatically in the last 25 years, and we have been borrowing much of the money needed to make that prosperity happen.

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:45.680
and so so when so when you see Congress sort of saying like we need to make sure that there are that there are strings attached to this money we need to make sure that the banks are lending it out that doesn't make any sense it makes not only no sense it makes reverse say it's nonsense sense, because what the banks have done is already lend too much.

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:48.820
The name of this problem is too much debt.

00:39:49.260 --> 00:40:00.960
I really think that's the heart of what's wrong with us, is that we have over borrowed, and we've done that over many, many decades, and now it's reached just an unbearable peak where people on average cannot repay the debts they've got.

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:06.080
In the face of that, it is no solution to try and lend more.

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:13.960
All right.

00:40:14.040 --> 00:40:18.120
So, Alex, our little radio drama is over.

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:24.360
We've laughed a little, cried a little, and we hope now that you will be able to understand the news.

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:27.860
Let's just talk here at the end about what the government is actually doing this week.

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:29.200
It's been a pretty dramatic week.

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:33.540
On Wednesday, the government revealed the details of its plan to save the banks.

00:40:33.670 --> 00:40:45.100
Now, remember we said that there are people out there who are saying the government has to act quickly to acknowledge the problems on bank balance sheets, declare insolvent banks insolvent, and step in aggressively to fix it all.

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:49.340
One person who believes that, Simon Johnson, that former IMF official.

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:53.720
Yeah, I think there's a level of deceit here, right, on the part of the banks.

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:56.900
And I think the government is going along with it far too much.

00:40:57.060 --> 00:41:04.100
which one of my colleagues nicely articulates what should be the principle now, which is no new lies.

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:10.960
The balance sheet of these banks is, as far as we know, a huge lie.

00:41:11.620 --> 00:41:16.140
They don't want to show you and tell themselves what this stuff is really worth.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:24.960
And we're saying deal with it now, deal with it seriously, confront the problems in the banking system openly, or there will be hell to pay.

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:32.640
Simon Johnson says, every day we delay taking over insolvent banks, the economy gets weaker and the solution gets more difficult.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:38.080
Well, according to the plan the Obama administration revealed this week, they have a different view.

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:41.000
They think it's best to go about things a bit more deliberately.

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:45.940
They announced on Wednesday they're going to take two months to figure out how healthy the banks are.

00:41:46.290 --> 00:41:48.420
That's the stress test you've heard a lot about.

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:52.140
And another six months to give some money to the banks that need it.

00:41:52.820 --> 00:42:00.840
And they've said to the media and to Congress, that they're pretty sure they know what those stress tests are going to find, that America's biggest banks are healthy.

00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:03.520
The government will never need to take them over.

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:23.480
In other words, given a choice between Simon's option, a scary and unprecedented, for this country at least, takeover of the nation's largest banks, and much smaller measures where they keep the banks afloat, don't challenge them too much on the truthfulness of their balance sheets, and hope that in time things will sort themselves out, they're going with time.

00:42:24.080 --> 00:42:26.800
But their public statements did leave them plenty of wiggle room.

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:32.120
And of course, if they were planning to take over the banking system, they wouldn't announce it beforehand.

00:42:32.840 --> 00:42:39.780
They'd probably say exactly what they're saying right now, at least until they got everything set up, until they hired enough people, put all their plans in order.

00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:45.520
And then one Friday evening, they'd make an announcement and nationalize the banks over the weekend.

00:42:53.480 --> 00:43:03.800
Alex Bloomberg and Adam Davidson, in addition to their radio jobs, they do a blog and a podcast three times a week where they explain the latest economic news in normal language that anybody can understand.

00:43:04.400 --> 00:43:05.480
This is the Planet Money podcast.

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.300
It's at npr.org slash money.

00:43:09.260 --> 00:43:16.460
Coming up after the break, we go on a field trip to visit a toxic asset in its natural environment, which in this case turns out to be suburban New Jersey.

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:21.380
That's in a minute for Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:21.580
news.

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:24.540
It's American Life from Her Glass.

00:43:25.420 --> 00:43:28.820
We've arrived at Act Two of our show, Act Two, Cleanup Squad.

00:43:29.680 --> 00:43:39.840
Well, as we heard earlier in today's program, in the end, solving the banking crisis means that somebody is just going to have to buy up these toxic assets, the stuff that is poisoning bank balance sheets and making them insolvent.

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:48.600
One of the main principles behind the Obama administration's strategy is that they're trying to get private investors to do as much of this buying up as possible.

00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:54.220
So the administration has created all kinds of incentives and guarantees for investors to do just that.

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:59.900
But private investors are also stepping in to buy toxic assets all over the country in much smaller ways.

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:04.600
And to understand this, take the simplest toxic asset there is, a home mortgage.

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:09.740
A home mortgage where the homeowners can't make their payments and they're in foreclosure or they're near foreclosure.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:12.940
There are guys around the country who are buying up those mortgages.

00:44:13.740 --> 00:44:16.380
Basically, this takes the mortgages off the bank's hands.

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:23.040
And imagine for a second, if that happened to your mortgage, one of these guys would now basically be just like the bank as far as you were concerned.

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:25.840
If you defaulted, that guy would get your house.

00:44:26.140 --> 00:44:30.400
If you wanted to renegotiate the terms of your mortgage so you don't default, you would do it with him.

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:34.540
Well, reporter Lisa Chow went out with a couple of these business people.

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:40.920
The first time I meet the guys who actually want more toxic assets in their lives is in a diner in New Jersey.

00:44:41.720 --> 00:44:44.880
Raj Bhatia is sitting at a corner booth with two other guys.

00:44:45.220 --> 00:44:48.840
My fellow Ivy League MBAs think I'm nuts because I'm going to get dirty.

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:50.760
I'm going to have to get dirty now, you know.

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:56.180
Raj graduated from Columbia Business School in the mid-90s, and for years he ran a hedge fund.

00:44:56.820 --> 00:45:01.940
And by getting dirty, he means he's actually going to be getting out in the world instead of sitting behind a desk.

00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:08.000
He's convinced he can make money buying home mortgages that aren't being paid back.

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:12.900
Raj has teamed up with Albert Baheen to buy pools of these mortgages.

00:45:13.340 --> 00:45:23.620
And we got together and basically said, you know, there's going to be some tremendous opportunities in buying some of this residential debt that I basically, you know, helped create over the past six, seven, eight years.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:32.720
Albert started selling mortgages in 2000, just as housing prices began to skyrocket and saw a lot of the abuses and terrible lending practices.

00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:35.200
He steered clear of most of that.

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:41.480
And now, as those mortgages are starting to go bad, Albert and Raj are looking to buy them up at a discount.

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:50.000
And they're meeting in diners like these, with potential investors like this guy, Benek Oster, who's made millions in finance over the years.

00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:52.140
Hypothetically, I've got $5 million.

00:45:52.480 --> 00:45:54.460
I'd like to put in, how much are you buying?

00:45:54.540 --> 00:45:55.670
How many loans are you getting for $5 million?

00:45:56.620 --> 00:45:58.300
I'm going to make it very easy for you.

00:45:58.660 --> 00:46:00.720
Average loan figure, $300,000 to $350,000.

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:03.280
Figure one-third of the price of that is no third.

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:11.840
I'm in a car with Raj, Albert, and This American Life producer Alex Bloomberg.

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:14.840
We're driving to a town in New Jersey called Wayne.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:17.100
The business pitch at the diner worked.

00:46:17.460 --> 00:46:22.320
Albert and Raj have a couple investors at this point, and they're getting ready to buy some mortgages.

00:46:23.460 --> 00:46:24.180
Here's how it works.

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:28.540
Banks have given lots and lots of mortgages to people who've stopped paying.

00:46:29.140 --> 00:46:40.880
To get them off their books, the banks have sold them at huge discounts, And they do it in massive numbers, thousands of mortgages at a time, to hedge funds and other large investment funds.

00:46:41.540 --> 00:46:43.900
These funds are sometimes called vulture funds.

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:46.840
They don't actually want to keep these mortgages.

00:46:47.320 --> 00:46:53.000
They just want to buy from the banks low, mark up the price a little, and sell to guys like Albert and Raj for a profit.

00:46:53.540 --> 00:46:55.260
Which is why we're on this trip.

00:46:55.980 --> 00:47:02.300
Albert and Raj are thinking of buying the mortgages for 40 houses from one of these funds, all homes in New Jersey.

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:04.320
and they want to go see what they're buying.

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:08.730
They've made more than a dozen of these trips so far to look at different homes.

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:12.760
And sometimes it feels a little weird, like they're spying or something.

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:13.840
I'll tell you a story.

00:47:13.880 --> 00:47:22.500
One of the first times Raj came out to see houses, I'd seen them a couple, I went out a couple times already, and we're just snapping a picture of the subject property, and Raj is like, there's a cop.

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:25.160
I'm like, we're not doing anything wrong.

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:27.700
We're just snapping a picture of a house.

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:31.140
This is totally new for you then, Raj.

00:47:31.140 --> 00:48:07.460
like you're not forever you're not well that's that's another good point nobody has done this yes big funds have bought a lot of product but to actually go home to home and reprice the mortgages and talk to homeowners this is all very very very new raj and albert know certain things about this group of loans they know that 90 of the houses are in foreclosure they know the balances on all the mortgages they know when the borrowers stop making payments they know what what the borrowers claimed in income on their original loan documents, although they also know enough not to believe those numbers too strongly.

00:48:08.200 --> 00:48:11.340
But there's lots of things the mortgage documents can't tell you.

00:48:11.580 --> 00:48:16.780
For instance, Albert and Raj don't even know if someone's living in the house they're about to go look at.

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:20.480
New Jersey's unusually slow to kick someone out of a house.

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:26.480
Even on loans that are 15 months delinquent, people can still be there waiting to be evicted.

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:28.680
And they wanna know other things.

00:48:29.140 --> 00:48:30.220
How does the neighborhood feel?

00:48:30.960 --> 00:48:33.120
And so far, this neighborhood feels good.

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:34.280
Nice neighborhood.

00:48:34.480 --> 00:48:38.920
You can imagine what this looks like in the summertime with all the canopy of trees.

00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:41.220
It's an established area.

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:44.560
I really am impressed with this.

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:46.860
I mean, this is a nice home.

00:48:47.020 --> 00:48:50.480
This is how I would grow up and raise a family in this neighborhood.

00:48:50.540 --> 00:48:50.960
This is great.

00:48:51.860 --> 00:48:53.800
And Albert notices another good sign.

00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:57.580
The thing we look for is there's really no for sale signs around, which is great.

00:48:57.790 --> 00:49:00.440
So there's not that much product out there in this neighborhood.

00:49:01.300 --> 00:49:02.300
This is great.

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:03.780
I've not seen a for sale sign.

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:06.480
You'll see one or two coming up, I'm sure, but not yet.

00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:09.320
Which means the market's not glutted.

00:49:09.400 --> 00:49:11.860
If they take over the house, they can sell it.

00:49:11.960 --> 00:49:17.820
And that's another thing they're doing, looking at how houses similar to the ones in their pool have sold recently.

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:22.120
We drive up to one house on a corner that recently got bought.

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:25.180
Albert pulls out a digital camera and takes a photo.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:26.460
Okay, run, run, run, go.

00:49:26.830 --> 00:49:27.400
No, I'm kidding.

00:49:28.940 --> 00:49:33.560
Then we drive around the corner to the house whose mortgage Albert and Raj are looking to own.

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:35.240
You have reached the destination.

00:49:35.660 --> 00:49:36.540
This is it right here.

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:38.400
All right, that's it on the right-hand side.

00:49:39.820 --> 00:49:41.120
Raj parks the car.

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:45.080
The house is a red, old-style colonial on a suburban street.

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:51.180
Albert notices that there are little stickers in the window alerting firefighters that children live here.

00:49:51.780 --> 00:49:52.730
The lawn is well manicured.

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:54.380
There's a fenced-in backyard.

00:49:55.680 --> 00:50:03.040
According to Albert's paperwork, the house's appraised value back in the fall of 2007 was $635,000.

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:12.060
Now it's down to $440,000, which is pretty bad if you consider that what they owe on the house is $475,000.

00:50:12.940 --> 00:50:20.720
They're underwater, they owe more than their house is worth, and they haven't made a mortgage payment in 16 months since October of 2007.

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:23.300
But you never know by looking at it.

00:50:25.180 --> 00:50:27.940
This is a neighborhood where you grow up a family.

00:50:28.180 --> 00:50:29.360
There's a backyard.

00:50:29.500 --> 00:50:31.240
This house is generally in good condition.

00:50:31.520 --> 00:50:33.800
You can definitely tell that someone still lives here.

00:50:34.120 --> 00:50:36.220
Some of the houses, no one lives in it at all.

00:50:37.180 --> 00:50:39.640
The loan documents don't tell you any of that.

00:50:40.420 --> 00:50:44.760
Albert and Raj say they're not actually allowed, by law, to talk to the people living here.

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:48.900
But once they close the deal and take possession of the mortgage, they can.

00:50:49.840 --> 00:50:54.520
And in fact, the better deal Raj and Albert get, the better it might be for the homeowner.

00:50:55.500 --> 00:50:56.680
Raj explains it this way.

00:50:57.640 --> 00:51:02.160
Say the mortgage is worth $400,000, but they get it at $200,000.

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:07.660
They can approach the homeowner and say, we can cut you a new deal, rewrite your mortgage.

00:51:08.360 --> 00:51:09.600
And then, for the homeowner...

00:51:10.600 --> 00:51:12.120
The numbers are very different.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:21.540
Their monthly payments can go down from $1,300 to $500 a month, and we are making an incredibly good return on our money.

00:51:22.220 --> 00:51:24.820
And you can do that because you've bought it at such a discount.

00:51:24.820 --> 00:51:29.620
Yeah, it's really not that we're using creative rocket science finance.

00:51:29.780 --> 00:51:36.720
We bought the mortgage at a reduction, and somebody's taken a hit on that, and we have the ability to restructure that mortgage now.

00:51:37.360 --> 00:51:45.300
But Albert says so far, in their experience, even with this kind of reduction, three-quarters of the homeowners can't afford to make the payments.

00:51:45.880 --> 00:51:47.860
So Raj says there's a second option.

00:51:48.460 --> 00:51:56.880
If they absolutely can't do something, then you proceed with the foreclosure, and right now we're in discussions to rent back the homes to the homeowners.

00:51:57.120 --> 00:52:00.820
Where they've lived there, they stay there, but they're renting the home now, they don't own it.

00:52:00.980 --> 00:52:04.760
And lots of times you would think that they would be resistant to that.

00:52:04.820 --> 00:52:05.200
They're not.

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:09.960
It's really, you know, it becomes almost a relief in a certain way.

00:52:11.240 --> 00:52:15.040
To understand that relief, imagine the family in this house.

00:52:15.600 --> 00:52:18.440
They owe about $475,000 to the bank.

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:26.210
Since the house is worth $440,000, even after they sold it, they'd still owe the bank $35,000.

00:52:27.400 --> 00:52:33.480
Albert and Raj would let them stay in the house, rent it at a reduced rent, and they'd wipe out all that debt.

00:52:34.260 --> 00:52:37.000
For Albert and Raj, this is a business.

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:39.780
They aren't doing this out of the kindness of their hearts.

00:52:40.220 --> 00:52:40.980
They want to make money.

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:44.090
They're buying homes in the process of foreclosure.

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:50.600
That process can draw out for months, which can be costly to Albert and Raj if the occupants aren't paying anything.

00:52:51.440 --> 00:52:58.500
So if Albert and Raj can't sell the house to the occupants for a lower price or get them to rent, they at least want them out.

00:52:59.140 --> 00:53:02.260
And in that situation, Raj has a simple pitch to the homeowners.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:07.530
Why don't we help you by writing you a check, $5,000 to $7,000?

00:53:07.860 --> 00:53:09.700
You know, every home is different, depends on percentages.

00:53:09.980 --> 00:53:13.760
And we'll help you move, and you just give us the keys and we'll take over the home.

00:53:13.760 --> 00:53:18.560
Albert and Raj have already brokered a couple of pools of mortgages for other investors.

00:53:19.480 --> 00:53:24.580
Raj says the best case scenario is the homeowners are willing to talk to them and renegotiate the loan.

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:30.260
Worst case scenario really isn't worst case, it's that we're not getting any communication from the homeowner.

00:53:30.440 --> 00:53:33.140
We try to call, we try to send letters.

00:53:33.720 --> 00:53:39.880
We really haven't encountered a homeowner coming out with a shotgun saying, get off my property.

00:53:40.340 --> 00:53:59.080
It strikes me that looking at what you guys are doing right now, so if this works, you buy this pool, you get the house price to what it needs to be, basically, and you get the homeowner into either a mortgage that they can afford or they're able to sort of rent it or you pay them a little bit so they can move somewhere else.

00:53:59.690 --> 00:54:00.790
You know, it's not great.

00:54:01.100 --> 00:54:06.680
Nothing about this process is great, but it actually sounds like what we're talking about here is a free market solution to the problem.

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:08.440
Right?

00:54:08.540 --> 00:54:08.880
Or not?

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:10.760
Yeah, that's exactly what it is.

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:12.960
It is, but I don't think this alone either will.

00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:18.420
I think that there's got to be some sort of government help along with this to clear this up.

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:18.860
But why is that?

00:54:18.980 --> 00:54:20.960
I mean, you did this with that government help, or did you not?

00:54:20.980 --> 00:54:21.540
I'll tell you why.

00:54:21.600 --> 00:54:22.740
It's a simple answer.

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:26.080
There's not enough of us out there to absorb what's out there.

00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:31.640
In other words, if we had unlimited funds, if mortgage strategies had unlimited funds.

00:54:31.900 --> 00:54:32.520
That's the name of your company.

00:54:32.660 --> 00:54:33.420
That's the name of the company.

00:54:33.420 --> 00:55:10.860
we could we could ramp up very quickly and we could be putting away hundreds of millions of dollars of this type of product and working through it I mean we we could do that we would have a staff and we'd be out there and we do it and that would maybe make a little bit of a dent here in the New York metro area you got to remember there's trillions of dollars of product out there right now But no, no, there's even a bigger issue out here, and what we're seeing is that a lot of banks don't want to get rid of this product because I think they can't afford to get rid of it at the prices that the market is saying, this is market.

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:21.120
Like in other words, if they sell you these pools of loans at the prices you need to be able to make money and actually do the work you're doing here, they'd go bankrupt.

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:22.260
Yeah, they'd be insolvent.

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:25.320
Yes, I think so.

00:55:27.400 --> 00:55:59.100
that's exactly what i'm saying here's another problem albert and raj as hard as they're working and as complicated as it seems are actually dealing with the simplest assets out there simple home loans between a bank and a person they're not dealing in the complex world of mortgage-backed securities where mortgages were bundled sliced and sold to thousands of investors and where it's difficult to trace ownership and almost impossible to figure out what you're actually buying and who you're buying it from.

00:55:59.380 --> 00:56:05.160
They're not dealing in any of the exotic financial instruments like collateralized debt obligations.

00:56:06.320 --> 00:56:13.160
Essentially, Albert and Raj are buying the least toxic of the toxic assets on banks' balance sheets.

